WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Transitioning from iOS to React Native can feel like a collision of worlds.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.920
Moving from the highly opinionated Swift ecosystem to a JavaScript framework

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.040
often carries a specific type of developer friction.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.160
It feels like you are abandoning structural discipline.

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.600
You worry about losing trusted patterns like MVVM,

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.880
Viper, and Clean Dependency Graphs in favor of an environment that appears unopinionated.

7
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.720
However, React Native supports and scales on these specific layers

8
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.960
and precise dependency injection.

9
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.360
While the syntax and vocabulary are different,

10
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.120
the separation of concerns follows a strikingly similar logic.

11
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.920
Because these underlying dependency graphs align,

12
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.680
your existing mental model as an iOS developer provides a direct map

13
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.840
for navigating React architecture.

14
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.040
We are going to map MVVM, the combined framework,

15
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.520
dependency injection, and your data layers directly to their React Native equivalents.

16
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.240
You do not need to relearn how to build software.

17
00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.840
You only need to identify how your Swift instincts translate to these new tools.

18
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.040
In SwiftUI, you build a V-model as a class

19
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.600
that publishes state changes using observable object and at-published.

20
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.920
The view subscribes to those changes,

21
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.920
re-renders when the state updates,

22
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.880
and does not execute network calls itself.

23
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.600
This pattern maps directly to its React Native counterpart, the Custom Hook.

24
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.000
To keep the architectural intent clear,

25
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.840
we use a naming convention like UseProductListVModel.

26
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.840
It makes the module's role easy to identify within the code base.

27
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.280
A key constraint here,

28
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.600
the Custom Hook must remain completely free of any JSX code.

29
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.600
By removing the UI components,

30
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.160
the hook functions as a headless data engine,

31
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.840
returning only state variables and executable callbacks.

32
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.200
Because it is headless,

33
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.160
you can write unit tests for the logic and isolation,

34
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.480
just as you would for a Swift class.

35
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.960
Utilizing Custom Hooks as view models

36
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.360
enforces a thin presentation layer,

37
00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.680
preventing the massive view controller issue

38
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.720
from migrating into your React Native project.

39
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.160
iOS developers often use monolithic combined pipelines

40
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.240
to map asynchronous network responses

41
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.800
directly into reactive view states.

42
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.600
Modern React Native architecture splits this pipeline

43
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.680
into two specialized pathways,

44
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.800
one for server state and one for client state.

45
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.120
The first pathway uses TANstack Query

46
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.760
to handle all asynchronous server state.

47
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.360
TANstack Query manages complex server tasks automatically,

48
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.240
including request de-duplication, background fetching,

49
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.680
and network retries.

50
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.080
This isolates network loading and error states

51
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.080
from your core business logic.

52
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.160
The second pathway uses ZooStand,

53
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.080
a dedicated engine for managing synchronous local UI state.

54
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.360
Structurally, a ZooStand store serves as the direct replacement

55
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.480
for at-published properties within the Swift ecosystem.

56
00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.120
It prevents unnecessary re-renders,

57
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.600
allowing views to select only these specific slices of state they need.

58
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.840
Separating volatile server cache from stable local UI state

59
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.360
provides more precise control over application reactivity

60
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.600
than a single combined pipeline.

61
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.560
In the data layer, the iOS repository pattern

62
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.000
abstract sources using URL session and codeable.

63
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.200
This transforms smoothly into the React Native equivalent,

64
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.400
a strictly typed TypeScript API client.

65
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.360
We use TypeScript generics to guarantee type safety

66
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.880
at the network boundary.

67
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.000
This serves the same function as Swift's codeable.

68
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
For managing module dependencies,

69
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.640
SwiftUI relies on at-environment object

70
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.400
for dependency injection.

71
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.920
The React equivalent for this behavior is ReactContext.

72
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.520
By wrapping feature modules inside ReactContext providers,

73
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.320
we can isolate and manage the specific dependencies

74
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.840
that tree requires.

75
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.800
This makes it simple to swap a production API provider

76
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.040
for a mock provider during testing.

77
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.160
This provider pattern follows the same interface contract

78
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.160
requirements found in Swift protocol-oriented programming.

79
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.120
Using ReactContext for dependency injection

80
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.080
ensures that modules remain as decoupled and unit

81
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.040
testable as any native Swift module.

82
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.520
Traditional iOS Viper structures are organized by horizontal layers,

83
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.640
grouping all views, interactors, and presenters together

84
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.480
across the entire app.

85
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.240
React Native developers typically

86
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.520
prefer a feature slice design, which

87
00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.800
organizes the code based vertically by feature domains.

88
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.360
Each feature domain uses a barrel export,

89
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.080
the index.ts file, as the gatekeeper for that folder.

90
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.000
The barrel export serves as the strict public interface

91
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.160
for the module.

92
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.160
It encapsulates the feature, hiding internal logic

93
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.960
and private files from the rest of the application.

94
00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.800
This prevents unauthorized cross-feature imports

95
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.840
and forces the code to respect domain boundaries.

96
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.160
By combining feature slice design with barrel exports,

97
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.520
you achieve the same module isolation

98
00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.760
that Viper mandates on iOS.

99
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.320
To summarize, this comprehensive mapping

100
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.160
shows how your architectural knowledge translates row by row.

101
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.040
View models become hooks.

102
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.280
At publish state becomes a stand.

103
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.760
At environment object becomes context.

104
00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:08.240
And Viper organization becomes feature slice design.

105
00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.800
The core principle remains identical.

106
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.720
Presentation components must always

107
00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.960
be thin, logic free, and purely visual.

108
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.760
Continue to push complex business and state logic

109
00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.320
into custom hooks.

110
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.440
Maintain your use of generics to ensure

111
00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.280
safety at the network edge.

112
00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.400
React Native does not require you to unlearn software

113
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.360
engineering.

114
00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.440
Your existing Swift instincts provide

115
00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.080
the logical foundation required

116
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.600
to build scalable cross-platform applications.

